<Project>
  <!--
    Keep MSBuildProjectDirectory out of the generated editorconfig file, so that
    a build with no changes does not recompile anything.

    No project in this repo uses the Web SDK yet, so today this target does
    nothing. It is here so that a Web SDK project added later is covered
    without anyone having to remember to add it.

    THE PROBLEM

    This is the same problem that the ProjectDir setting in Directory.Build.props
    fixes; read the comment there first. In short: "dotnet build" run from a
    terminal sees the repo's path as "C:\...", while the C# Dev Kit in VS Code
    sees it as "c:\...", and its background design-time builds use that path.
    Both write the project's path into
    obj/<Configuration>/<TargetFramework>/<Project>.GeneratedMSBuildEditorConfig.editorconfig,
    that file is an input to the compiler, and so each change of the drive
    letter's case makes the next "dotnet build" recompile the project.

    Projects that use the Web SDK (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web) write the path a
    second time. The Web SDK includes the Razor SDK, whose
    _PrepareRazorSourceGenerators target (in
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor.SourceGenerators.targets) adds, among others,

      <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="MSBuildProjectDirectory" />

    so the editorconfig also gets a line such as

      build_property.MSBuildProjectDirectory = C:\Users\me\dev\monocle\apps\MyWebApp

    Its drive letter changes case in the same way as ProjectDir's. But
    MSBuildProjectDirectory is a reserved property: MSBuild sets it from the
    project file's path and reports error MSB4004 if a project tries to set it.
    So it cannot be given an upper-case drive letter as ProjectDir is.

    THE FIX

    Instead of normalizing the value, this target removes the property from the
    editorconfig. Only the Razor source generator reads
    build_property.MSBuildProjectDirectory, and it needs it only to process
    .razor and .cshtml files. No project in this repo has any, so the generator
    has nothing to process.

    The removal has to happen in a target, not in a top-level ItemGroup, because
    the Razor SDK adds the item in a target, which runs after all top-level items
    are evaluated. The ordering works like this:

    - GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFile (in Roslyn's Microsoft.Managed.Core.targets)
      depends on GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileShouldRun and then on
      GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileCore, which writes the file from the
      CompilerVisibleProperty items.
    - _PrepareRazorSourceGenerators runs before
      GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileShouldRun, and adds the item.
    - This target runs before GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileCore, and so after
      the item is added but before the file is written.

    In projects that do not use the Web SDK, there is no such item, and the
    Remove does nothing.

    IF YOU ADD RAZOR FILES

    If a project gains .razor or .cshtml files, the Razor source generator will
    need build_property.MSBuildProjectDirectory. Make this target skip that
    project (for example, with a Condition on the project's name), and accept
    that the project will recompile more often than it needs to while VS Code
    has the folder open.
  -->
  <Target Name="DropCaseVaryingCompilerVisibleProperties"
          BeforeTargets="GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileCore">
    <ItemGroup>
      <CompilerVisibleProperty Remove="MSBuildProjectDirectory" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>
</Project>
